2. ELIGIBILITY REQUIREMENTS (POL-BONAFIDE)

2.1 Active Enrollment Status: The applicant must be currently enrolled with active matriculation status in UniCampus ERP;
2.2 Fee Arrears Clearance: Zero total tuition fee defaults or outstanding financial hold flags on PayVarsity;
2.3 Disciplinary Standing: Students undergoing active disciplinary suspension, debarment, or inquiries before the Proctorial Board (STU-POL-005) are strictly ineligible for bonafide certification.

3. APPLICATION SUBMISSION PROCEDURE (PROC-BONAFIDE-REQ)

3.1 Digital Submission Channel: Students initiate the request digitally via UniCampus ERP by navigating to: Student Services > Certificates > 'General Bonafide Certificate Request' (STU-FRM-001);
3.2 Required Form Fields: Full Student Name, Roll / Registration Number, Degree Program & School, Current Semester, Date of Birth, Father's / Mother's Name, Permanent Home Address, and Explicit Purpose of Request (e.g., Regional Passport Office, Nationalised Bank, Transit Authority);
3.3 Document Uploads: Where mandated by external authorities (such as passport appointment schedules or visa verification notices), an official requirement letter must be attached as a PDF.

4. PROCESSING TIMELINE AND ISSUANCE SLA

4.1 Standard SLA: General bonafide certificates are generated digitally within two (2) working days following submission;
4.2 Digital Authentication: Certificates are issued as tamper-evident, digitally signed PDF documents bearing an official university seal, Registrar signature facsimile, and a unique verifiable dynamic QR code linked to the public UniCampus verification portal;
4.3 Hard Copy Attestation: Where embossed physical ink stamps are required by foreign embassies or statutory bodies, students present the printed PDF at the Registrar Services Counter (Chanakya Bhavan, Ground Floor Visitor Hub) for embossing.

2. SCOPE OF ESTIMATED FINANCIAL COMPONENTS

The resulting certified statement encapsulates complete, multi-year projected institutional expenditures across the remaining academic duration:
- Annual Tuition Fees and Specialized Laboratory / Studio Charges;
- Mandatory University Registration, Examination, and Convocation Fees;
- Institutional Caution Deposits (Refundable under FIN-POL-003);
- Standard Residential Hostel Accommodation and Dining Mess Fees (where applicable);
- Standard Book Bank and Academic Resource Surcharges.

3. APPLICATION AND SUBMISSION WORKFLOW

3.1 Submission: The student accesses UniCampus ERP > Student Services > 'Education Loan Fee Estimation Request' (STU-FRM-002);
3.2 Applicant Information: Student enters Lending Bank Name, Branch IFSC Code, Loan Account / File Number, and Residential Status (Day Scholar vs Hostel Resident);
3.3 Supporting Attachments: Student uploads the official Bank Loan Sanction In-Principle Letter or Bank Fee Schedule Proforma.

4. VERIFICATION WORKFLOW AND ISSUANCE SLA

4.1 Multi-Stage Audit: Academic Affairs validates active progression and program duration (e.g., 4 years for B.Tech). Finance and Accounts reconciles fee ledgers and inserts official financial fee tables;
4.2 Processing Timeline: Specialized fee estimation statements require four (4) working days from digital application;
4.3 Issuance Format: Hard copy printed on official university security letterhead, jointly endorsed with wet-ink signatures and embossed seals of the Student Fee Accounts Officer (DEPT-FIN) and University Registrar (DEPT-ADM);
4.4 Collection Point: Dispatched via Registrar Services Counter, Chanakya Bhavan, Ground Floor Visitor Hub.

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY DISAMBIGUATION (TC AND LC)

2.1 Transfer Certificate (TC under DEPT-ADM / STU-FRM-003 / ADM-POL-004): The official discharge certificate detailing date of admission, date of leaving, academic conduct, and release from university jurisdiction. Must not be confused with 'Training Coordinator' (TC under DEPT-CPTD / CPTD-STF-001);
2.2 Leaving Certificate (LC under DEPT-ADM / ADM-POL-004): The colloquial and regional statutory synonym for the institutional discharge document. Must not be confused with 'Library Committee' (LC under DEPT-LIB / LIB-GOV-001).

3. MANDATORY PRE-CONDITIONS AND NO-DUES CLEARANCE (ADM-SOP-001)

No application for a Transfer Certificate or Migration Certificate can be processed until 100% completion of the University Institutional No-Dues Clearance workflow on UniCampus ERP:
- Clearance of library book loans and circulation fines (DEPT-LIB);
- Hostel room vacation sign-off and key surrender (DEPT-HSTL);
- Engineering lab hardware component reconciliation (Academic Departments);
- Full settlement of tuition and campus accounts ledgers (DEPT-FIN).

4. APPLICATION PROCEDURE AND DISPATCH TIMELINE

4.1 Online Form Lodging: Following No-Dues clearance, the applicant accesses UniCampus ERP > Student Services > 'TC & Migration Request' (STU-FRM-003);
4.2 Applicant Submissions: Student enters Reason for Departure (Graduation / Admission to Another Institution / Personal Withdrawal), Destination University Name, and Verified Mailing Postal Address;
4.3 Dispatch SLA: Certificates are signed by the Registrar, embossed with the official University Common Seal, and ready for physical collection or registered speed-post dispatch within seven (7) working days;
4.4 Duplicate Certificate Norms: A duplicate TC is never issued routinely; applicants must submit an authenticated police non-traceable certificate and an attested notary affidavit.

3. STATUTORY LIMITS AND SUBMISSION RULES

3.1 Ten-Day Semester Cap: A student is entitled to a maximum cumulative limit of ten (10) working days of On-Duty leave per academic semester across all courses;
3.2 Pre-Event Submission SLA: Applications must be submitted on ResolveDesk (Category: Student Affairs > On-Duty Sanction) at least three (3) working days prior to departure;
3.3 Mandatory Pre-Event Documentation: Official invitation brochure, match fixture/event schedule, and formal faculty endorsement letter;
3.4 Post-Event Verification SLA: Within five (5) working days of returning to campus, the student must upload the verified Certificate of Participation or Merit Certificate;
3.5 Attendance Adjustment: AttendEdge programmatically converts absent session stamps for sanctioned dates into 'Excused Present (OD)' for SEE hall ticket calculation.

2. STATUTORY BOUNDARIES AND ABSOLUTE NON-CONDONABILITY

2.1 Condonable Range: Discretionary condonation by the Dean of Academic Affairs applies strictly to attendance between 65.0% and 74.9%;
2.2 Sub-65% Prohibition: Attendance below 65.0% cannot be condoned under any circumstance; students with attendance < 65% are summarily detained ('W'/'SA' grade);
2.3 Submission Deadline: This form and supporting medical proofs must be lodged on ResolveDesk at least five (5) working days prior to the commencement of Semester End Examinations.

3. MANDATORY SUPPORTING DOCUMENTATION

3.1 Clinical Proofs: Detailed hospital discharge summary, physician consultation notes, registered medical practitioner prescriptions, and diagnostic lab reports;
3.2 Health Centre Verification Slip: Prior to lodging this form, the student must report in person to the Campus Health Centre within three (3) days of resuming classes. The Senior Chief Medical Officer examines documentation and issues an authenticated University Medical Verification Slip bearing a unique validation code;
3.3 Endorsement Routing: Faculty Advisor and HOD review course attendance records on AttendEdge and digitally endorse the application;
3.4 Detention Waiving: Dean's digital approval lifts the examination detention flag in UniCampus ERP, unblocking hall ticket downloads on ExamShield.

2. FEE SCHEDULE AND TIMELINES (FIN-POL-002)

2.1 Answer Script Digital Viewing: Application lodged on ExamShield within seven (7) calendar days of result publication; fee of Rs. 400 per course script;
2.2 Arithmetic Re-totalling: Application lodged within seven (7) days; fee of Rs. 300 per paper (processing SLA: 3 working days);
2.3 Formal Academic Re-evaluation: Application lodged within seven (7) days of viewing the answer script; fee of Rs. 1,200 per paper (processing SLA: 15 working days).

3. RE-EVALUATION WORKFLOW AND ARBITRATION NORMS (REL-010)

3.1 Blind Re-assessment: Original evaluator markings and scores are digitally masked; the script is reassessed independently by an external senior examiner;
3.2 15% Deviation Threshold: If the revised mark differs by >= 15% from the original score, the script is automatically referred to a third neutral senior evaluator for binding arbitration;
3.3 Grade Finality: The grade finalized after re-evaluation supersedes the earlier grade, even if the resulting mark is lower;
3.4 50% Fee Refund: If re-evaluation results in an upward mark revision of >= 10%, a 50% refund (Rs. 600) is credited to the student's PayVarsity account.

2. REPLACEMENT PROCEDURE AND TIMELINE

2.1 Loss Incident Logging: Student logs into ResolveDesk and submits this form under Category: Security > Lost ID Card. CampusPass immediately revokes the lost RFID chip serial number across all turnstiles;
2.2 Fee Payment: Student remits the prescribed non-refundable replacement fee of Rs. 500 online via PayVarsity (FIN-POL-002);
2.3 Physical Verification: Student presents the PayVarsity fee payment receipt and a recognized government photo identity card at the Central Security Office (Main Entrance Gate Security Complex);
2.4 Re-issuance SLA: The duplicate Smart RFID card is printed, encoded, and handed over within forty-eight (48) hours of fee settlement;
2.5 Automated Re-synchronization: CampusPass propagates the new RFID chip identifier to the Valmiki Central Library (LibNet), hostel biometric speed gates (DormTrack), and campus commuter buses (TransPass).

2. FIVE-DEPARTMENT CLEARANCE SIGN-OFF MATRIX

The digital No-Dues clearance workflow is executed strictly through UniCampus ERP and requires sequential sign-offs across five operational divisions:
2.1 Department 1: Central Library (DEPT-LIB / LIB-POL-001): Verifies the return of all circulating books, inter-library loans, and clearance of overdue fine ledgers on LibNet;
2.2 Department 2: Hostel Administration (DEPT-HSTL / HSTL-REG-001): Conducts room inventory inspection, verifies room key surrender, and audits mess bill ledgers on DormTrack;
2.3 Department 3: Parent Academic Department & Labs (DEPT-CSE, DEPT-ECE, etc.): Department lab in-charge verifies the return of all issued hardware development kits, microcontrollers, and clears accidental breakage slips (REL-018);
2.4 Department 4: Campus Operations & Sports (DEPT-OPS / DEPT-SAC): Confirms return of athletic equipment and lockers at the Student Activity Centre;
2.5 Department 5: Finance and Accounts Office (DEPT-FIN / FIN-POL-003): Audits entire multi-year fee ledger on PayVarsity, resolves outstanding tuition or transport arrears, authorizes the release of the refundable caution deposit, and issues final fiscal clearance.

3. SEQUENTIAL DIGITAL WORKFLOW IN UNICAMPUS ERP

3.1 Step 1 (Initiation): Student logs into UniCampus ERP > Student Services > 'Institutional No-Dues Clearance' during the designated graduation audit window (May-June);
3.2 Step 2 (Automated Verification): ERP queries LibNet and DormTrack database APIs to identify outstanding liabilities;
3.3 Step 3 (Departmental Queue Routing): Clearances route automatically to departmental dashboards for designated officer digital sign-off (SLA: 5 working days);
3.4 Step 4 (Caution Deposit Credit): Finance Office initiates automated electronic fund transfer (NEFT) of the verified caution deposit (FIN-POL-003) to the student's verified bank account within fourteen (14) working days;
3.5 Step 5 (Credential Release): Examination Cell generates official consolidated mark transcripts and provisional degree certificates; Admissions Office dispatches Transfer and Migration certificates.

2. CATEGORIES AND SERVICE-LEVEL AGREEMENTS (SLAS)

2.1 Category 1: General Bonafide Study Certificate (SLA: 2 Working Days): Issued for public bus transit concessions, regional passport office appointments, student software licenses, and competitive examination registrations;
2.2 Category 2: Specialized Loan Fee Estimation Letter (SLA: 4 Working Days): Formulated for commercial bank educational loans, detailing institutional tuition, hostel fees, and caution deposits;
2.3 Category 3: Visa / Embassy Sponsorship Attestation (SLA: 4 Working Days): Formulated for international student visa renewals or student travel visas requiring formal university sponsorship statements.

3. APPLICATION PROCEDURE VIA UNICAMPUS ERP

3.1 Digital Request Submission: Students log in to UniCampus ERP, navigate to Student Services > Certificates, select the appropriate certificate type, enter the specific recipient statutory authority, and upload prerequisite requirement letters;
3.2 Automated System Auditing: The ERP engine validates active enrollment, verifies zero active disciplinary suspension flags, and checks for financial blocks on PayVarsity;
3.3 Digital Certificate Generation: Approved general certificates are issued as downloadable PDF files bearing a secure, verifiable university cryptographic QR code;
3.4 Embossed Wet-Ink Attestation: When wet-ink stamps or wax seals are demanded by foreign consulates or banks, students present the printed certificate at Counter 2, Chanakya Bhavan Visitor Hub.

4. CERTIFICATE ISSUANCE AND DISPATCH TIMELINE (REL-015)

4.1 Processing Timeline: Following successful No-Dues clearance and submission of STU-FRM-003, statutory certificates are prepared within seven (7) working days;
4.2 Official Sealing: Certificates are formally signed by the University Registrar and embossed with the metal common seal;
4.3 Delivery Methods: Graduating students may collect certificates in person at the Registrar Counter (Chanakya Bhavan) upon surrendering their student RFID smart card, or request delivery via registered speed post upon settling postal courier fees on PayVarsity;
4.4 Duplicate Certificate Protocol: If an original TC or Migration Certificate is lost or destroyed, a duplicate can only be issued upon physical submission of an official police non-traceable report, an attested legal notary affidavit, and a duplicate fee of Rs. 1,000 paid via PayVarsity.

3. OFFICIAL SPOKESPERSON AND EXTERNAL STATEMENTS

3.1 Authorized Spokesperson: Only the Vice-Chancellor, Registrar, or designated Public Relations Officer (PRO) is authorized to issue formal institutional statements, respond to journalistic inquiries, or address press conferences;
3.2 Prohibition of Unauthorized Statements: Individual faculty members, students, club organizers, or staff are strictly prohibited from issuing official statements to print, broadcast, or online media on behalf of Demo University without written authorization from the Director of Media Relations.

4. PRESS RELEASE WORKFLOW FOR CAMPUS EVENTS

4.1 Advance Vetting: Student clubs and academic departments organizing major conferences, hackathons, or cultural fests seeking media publicity must draft a proposed press release and submit it via ResolveDesk to Media Relations at least five (5) working days prior to the event;
4.2 Photographic and Camera Permissions: External television crews, photojournalists, and documentary filmmakers must obtain signed media gate passes from the PR Office, verified by Security (SEC-POL-003) at the Main Entrance Gate.

3. ADMINISTRATIVE DESK HIERARCHY AND FUNCTIONS

3.1 University Registrar (ROLE-REG): Chief administrative and statutory executive; legal signatory for all university contracts, degrees, and regulatory affiliations;
3.2 Deputy Registrar (Administration): Manages campus human resources, ministerial personnel allocation, and estate governance;
3.3 Office Superintendent (OS): Floor in-charge of the Ground Floor Administrative Wing, Chanakya Bhavan. Supervises counter operations, verifies student certificate files, and certifies formal seal applications;
3.4 Counter Services Staff: Front-facing clerks operating designated service counters (Counter 1: Admissions & Bonafide; Counter 2: Fees & Challans; Counter 3: Examinations & Mark Sheets; Counter 4: Degree & TC Dispatch).

2. MANDATORY FRRO REGISTRATION (FORM S PROTOCOL)

2.1 14-Day Mandatory Window: Every foreign national student holding a student visa must complete online registration on the government e-FRRO portal within fourteen (14) days of arrival in the country;
2.2 Form S Submission: The International Student Advisor verifies passport credentials and submits digital institutional endorsement ('Form S') through the national immigration portal;
2.3 Address Proof: The student is issued an official certified Residential Bonafide Certificate by Hostel Administration (DEPT-HSTL / DormTrack) validating on-campus dormitory residency.

3. STUDENT VISA EXTENSION WORKFLOW

Students requiring visa renewals or extension endorsements must execute the following protocol at least sixty (60) days prior to visa expiration:
3.1 Application on ERP: The student logs into UniCampus ERP > International Services > 'Visa Extension Recommendation';
3.2 Academic Progression Clearance: Academic Affairs validates active academic progression, current semester attendance >= 75%, and zero pending disciplinary proceedings;
3.3 Formal Recommendation Letter: The International Admissions Office prepares a formal institutional Visa Extension Recommendation Letter signed by the Registrar, detailing degree duration, anticipated graduation year, and financial bona fides;
3.4 Police Liaison: The Campus Security Department (DEPT-SEC) assists local law enforcement and intelligence officers conducting mandatory statutory background checks.

2. STATUTORY ELIGIBILITY CONSTRAINTS

2.1 Active Matriculation: Bonafide certification is issued exclusively to students maintaining active matriculated enrollment in good standing;
2.2 Disciplinary Bar: Students facing active disciplinary suspension, debarment, or pending inquiries before the Proctorial Board (STU-POL-005) cannot be issued bonafide certificates;
2.3 Financial Default Bar: Total default of semester tuition fees blocks the generation of bonafide letters until the financial hold is liquidated in PayVarsity.

3. CLASSIFICATION OF CERTIFICATES AND PROCESSING SLAS

3.1 Standard Bonafide Certificate (SLA: 2 Working Days): Attests to active study for transit passes, passport verification, or student concessions. Issued as a tamper-evident digital PDF with a cryptographic QR code;
3.2 Specialized Financial Bonafide (SLA: 4 Working Days): Detailed fee structure letter formulated for bank education loans, containing certified tuition, hostel, and fee schedules verified by the Finance Office;
3.3 Visa and Embassy Sponsorship Letters (SLA: 4 Working Days): Specialized endorsements for consular authorities.

2. REGULATORY REQUIREMENTS AND STATUTORY CONTENTS

2.1 Mandatory Contents: In accordance with statutory education regulations, the Transfer Certificate records: Student Full Legal Name, Enrollment Number, Date of Initial Admission, Date of Leaving, Academic Degree Program, General Moral Conduct, and Reason for Departure;
2.2 Mandatory No-Dues Clearance: Application for TC and Migration must be accompanied by comprehensive institutional No-Dues clearance across Library, Hostels, Laboratories, and Accounts (ADM-SOP-001);
2.3 Issuance Timeline: Transfer and Migration certificates are prepared, sealed by the Registrar, and dispatched within seven (7) working days following successful No-Dues verification.

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (EC)

Within Demo University systems and communications, the abbreviation 'EC' possesses three completely distinct institutional meanings:
2.1 Executive Council (EC under Governance / GOV-ACT-001): The apex governing board and policy-making tribunal chaired by the Vice-Chancellor;
2.2 Examination Cell (EC under DEPT-EXAM / EXAM-GUI-005): The confidential evaluation and testing administration office headed by the Controller of Examinations;
2.3 Electronics and Communication Engineering (EC under DEPT-ECE / ECE-CRS-001): The academic engineering department and degree branch.
- Disambiguation Rule: Text referencing university-wide fiscal resolutions, statute amendments, or Board of Management directives refers to the Executive Council (GOV-ACT-001).

3. COUNCIL COMPOSITION AND MEMBERSHIP

The Executive Council comprises:
3.1 Chairperson: The Vice-Chancellor (ROLE-VC), presiding officer;
3.2 Secretary: The University Registrar (ROLE-REG), ex-officio member-secretary;
3.3 Academic Deans: Dean of Academic Affairs, Dean of Research, Dean of Student Welfare;
3.4 Chief Finance Officer (ROLE-CFO): Financial advisor;
3.5 External Nominees: Eminent educationalists, industrial leaders, and state government representatives.

4. POWERS, AUTHORITIES, AND FUNCTIONS

The Executive Council possesses statutory powers to:
- Ratify the annual fiscal budget, fee schedules (FIN-POL-001), and audited balance sheets;
- Create academic departments, research centers, and professorial chairs;
- Approve all major campus infrastructure investments and estate expansions;
- Serve as the final appellate authority for severe employee and student disciplinary expulsions.

4. POWERS, AUTHORITIES, AND FUNCTIONS

The Academic Council is empowered to:
- Enact and amend General Academic Regulations (ACAD-REG-001) and Postgraduate Regulations (ACAD-REG-002);
- Approve new degree programs, departmental course syllabi, and prerequisite structures;
- Formulate grading scales, relative curve formulas, and examination passing criteria;
- Appoint the Standing Committee of the Academic Council to adjudicate extraordinary academic appeals.

3. ELIGIBLE APPEALS AND PETITION TIMELINES

3.1 Contested Administrative Detentions: Extraordinary appeals contesting course detention where demonstrated administrative error or system failure occurred;
3.2 Compassionate Degree Time-Limit Extensions: Petitions by candidates exceeding standard N + 2 residency windows due to documented medical trauma;
3.3 Petition Lodging: Appeals must be submitted via ResolveDesk (Category: Academic Affairs > Senate Appeal) within seven (7) working days of the contested notification.

3. CORE LEAVE CATEGORIES AND ACCRUALS

3.1 Casual Leave (CL): 12 days per calendar year for full-time regular employees;
3.2 Earned Leave (EL): Accrued at statutory rates for non-vacation staff;
3.3 Academic Duty Leave (OD): Sanctioned for presenting research papers, attending statutory conferences, or external examination duties.

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (DOR)

Within university administrative nomenclature, the abbreviation 'DOR' designates three distinct institutional concepts:
2.1 Department Operational Review (DOR under IQAC / IQAC-REV-003): The annual quality audit evaluating academic departments on curriculum delivery, student outcomes, and research;
2.2 Date of Registration (DOR under DEPT-ACAD / ACAD-NOT-002): The mandatory deadline for semester course enrollment in UniCampus ERP;
2.3 Dean of Research (DOR under DEPT-RES / RES-OFF-001): The executive officer heading the research and doctoral administration office.
- Disambiguation Rule: Text referencing internal audits, accreditation checklists, or quality reviews refers to Department Operational Review (IQAC-REV-003).

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (IR)

Within Demo University administrative documentation, the abbreviation 'IR' represents three distinct entities:
2.1 Office of International Relations (IR under DEPT-IRO / IRO-POL-001): The administrative division overseeing study abroad mobility, international dual degrees, and foreign memoranda of understanding;
2.2 Institutional Repository (IR under DEPT-LIB / LIB-POL-004): The digital research library archive holding dissertations, theses, and publications;
2.3 Incident Report (IR under DEPT-SEC / SEC-SOP-003): The formal security documentation protocol logging campus thefts or safety incidents.
- Disambiguation Rule: Text referencing foreign universities, visas, exchange semesters, or overseas partner institutions maps strictly to International Relations (IRO-POL-001).

3. ELIGIBILITY AND APPLICATION WORKFLOW

3.1 Eligibility: Open to undergraduate students who have completed four semesters with a CGPA >= 7.50 and zero active backlogs;
3.2 Application: Students submit applications through UniCampus ERP attaching statements of purpose and language proficiency scores;
3.3 Credit Transfer: Pre-approved course syllabi transfer directly into Demo University academic records upon official transcript receipt.

3. APPROVAL WORKFLOW AND SAFETY MANDATES

3.1 Prior Sanction: Department field trip coordinators must submit an itinerary, risk assessment, and transport requisition via ResolveDesk at least fifteen (15) working days prior to the excursion;
3.2 Mandatory Parental Consent: Every participating student must submit a signed Parental Consent Form prior to departure;
3.3 Faculty Chaperones: A minimum ratio of one faculty member per twenty (20) students, including designated female faculty chaperones for co-educational cohorts, is mandatory.

6. INSTITUTIONAL NO-DUES CLEARANCE AND INTER-DEPARTMENTAL DEPENDENCIES (REL-006)

Complete clearance of all borrowed books and zero outstanding circulation fine balances in LibNet is mandatory for:
- Generation of Semester End Examination (SEE) hall tickets on ExamShield;
- Semester course registration approvals on UniCampus ERP;
- Formal institutional No-Dues clearance for degree certificate issuance, Transfer Certificate (TC) release, and caution deposit refund under FIN-POL-003 and ACAD-SOP-001.

2. LICENSED ELECTRONIC SCHOLARLY COLLECTIONS

Demo University subscribes to premier international peer-reviewed research databases to support undergraduate coursework, postgraduate dissertations, and sponsored doctoral research, including:
- IEEE Xplore Digital Library (Electrical, Electronics, and Computer Science);
- ScienceDirect / Elsevier (Engineering, Physical Sciences, Life Sciences);
- ACM Digital Library (Computing Machinery and Software Systems);
- SpringerLink, Scopus, and Web of Science (Multidisciplinary citation databases).

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (IR)

Within the Demo University enterprise ecosystem, the term 'IR' possesses three distinct, non-overlapping meanings across departments:
2.1 Institutional Repository (IR under DEPT-LIB / LIB-POL-004): The digital research archive preserving faculty publications, master dissertations, and doctoral theses;
2.2 Office of International Relations (IR under DEPT-IRO / IRO-POL-001): The administrative division facilitating student semester exchange programs, foreign dual degrees, and international memoranda of understanding (MOUs);
2.3 Incident Report (IR under DEPT-SEC / SEC-SOP-003): The security documentation protocol logging physical theft, safety infractions, or gate trespassing incidents on campus.

3. MANDATORY DEPOSIT SCOPE AND COVERAGE

Deposit in the Institutional Repository is an absolute prerequisite for degree completion and research sign-off across all academic programs:
3.1 Doctoral Ph.D. Theses: Final approved Ph.D. dissertations submitted under PROG-PHD-ENG and RES-POL-001;
3.2 Master Dissertations: M.Tech dissertation projects (e.g., CSE580 Phase I & II) and MBA master research theses;
3.3 Undergraduate Capstone Projects: Outstanding B.Tech capstone project reports recommended by departmental project evaluation committees;
3.4 Faculty Scholarly Works: Pre-prints, post-prints, conference proceedings, and peer-reviewed journal papers authored by Demo University faculty members.

2. CATALOG SEARCH MODES AND STACK DISCOVERY

2.1 Basic Catalog Search: Users can query the repository by entering keywords, book titles, author surnames, or ISBNs in the primary LibNet search bar.
2.2 Advanced Filtering Options: Search results can be filtered by publication year, department collection (e.g., Computer Science, Electrical, Mechanical, Management), language, and resource type (Print Book, E-Book, Bound Periodical, Dissertation).
2.3 Interpreting Stack Availability and Shelf Locations:
- Status 'Available': Indicates the volume is present on the shelf; the display shows the Floor Number, Stacks Aisle, and Dewey Decimal Call Number (e.g., Floor 2, Aisle 4B, Call No: 005.133 COR);
- Status 'Issued': Displays current loan status and projected due date of return;
- Status 'Reference Only': Restricted strictly to in-house reading room consultation;
- Status 'On Reserve': Held temporarily at the circulation reserve counter for specific academic courses.

3. BOOK RESERVATION AND HOLD PLACEMENT (PROC-LIB-CIRCULATION)

When a required book is currently issued to another borrower, registered patrons can place an automated reservation hold:
3.1 Hold Placement: Click 'Place Hold' on the catalog entry and authenticate using student ERP credentials;
3.2 Queue Prioritization: Holds are queued strictly on a first-come, first-served basis;
3.3 Return Alert and 48-Hour Collection Window: Upon physical return of the reserved book, LibNet dispatches an automated email/SMS alert to the reserving patron and places the volume in the 'Hold Shelf' at the Circulation Desk. The book is reserved exclusively for forty-eight (48) hours; if not collected within 48 hours, the hold expires and transfers to the next user in queue or returns to open circulation.

4. ONLINE BOOK RENEWAL WORKFLOW

Patrons may renew borrowed circulating items from any personal device without carrying books back to the library counter:
4.1 Step 1: Log in to https://libnet.demo.edu.internal/opac using institutional Single Sign-On (SSO);
4.2 Step 2: Navigate to 'My Account' > 'Current Checkouts';
4.3 Step 3: Identify the book to extend and click 'Renew Selected';
4.4 Conditions for Renewal Approval:
- The renewal request must be submitted prior to the original due date;
- No other patron has placed an active reservation hold on the item;
- The patron's cumulative unpaid fine balance in LibNet does not exceed Rs. 50;
- The book has not exceeded the maximum limit of two (2) consecutive online renewals.

5. RFID SELF-CHECKOUT KIOSK OPERATING INSTRUCTIONS

Patrons can borrow and return materials autonomously using self-service kiosks located in the Ground Floor Circulation Foyer:
5.1 Tap Student ID: Place your Demo University Smart RFID ID card against the contactless reader on the kiosk console;
5.2 Place Books on Reader Pad: Position up to four (4) books simultaneously on the RFID sensor surface;
5.3 Verify Screen Display: Ensure all book titles, barcodes, and computed due dates appear correctly on the touchscreen display;
5.4 Complete Checkout: Select 'Confirm Borrowing' and choose to print a paper receipt or send a digital receipt to your student email; the RFID security gate sensor is automatically deactivated for thirty (30) seconds to enable exit without alarm triggers.

3. COMMITTEE COMPOSITION AND MEMBERSHIP

The Library Advisory Committee comprises ten (10) members appointed for a two-year tenure:
3.1 Chairperson: A senior full Professor appointed by the Vice-Chancellor upon recommendation of the Dean of Academic Affairs;
3.2 Member Secretary: The University Chief Librarian (ROLE-LIB), serving as ex-officio executive convener;
3.3 Faculty Representatives: One tenured faculty member nominated from each Academic School:
- School of Computing and Data Sciences (SCH-SCDS);
- School of Electrical and Electronics Engineering (SCH-SEEE);
- School of Mechanical and Infrastructure Engineering (SCH-SMIE);
- School of Management and Commerce (SCH-SMC);
3.4 Administrative Ex-Officio Members:
- Director of IT Infrastructure (DEPT-IT) to coordinate enterprise bandwidth and LibNet integration;
- Chief Finance Officer / Bursar (DEPT-FIN) to oversee fiscal budget execution;
3.5 Student Representatives: One nominated doctoral research scholar and one postgraduate student representative selected on academic merit to communicate patron requirements.

4. POWERS, AUTHORITIES, AND ADVISORY FUNCTIONS

The Library Committee is empowered to:
4.1 Electronic Database and Journal Allocations: Review and approve annual renewal proposals for high-value electronic research subscriptions (IEEE Xplore, ScienceDirect, ACM Digital Library, Scopus) based on usage metrics provided by LibNet;
4.2 Departmental Book Procurement Grants: Formulate formulas for equitable distribution of book procurement funds across engineering and management departments;
4.3 Circulation Policies and Fine Schedules: Formulate recommendations for modifying borrowing quotas, overdue fine rates, and account lockout thresholds under POL-LIBRARY;
4.4 Infrastructure Expansion: Approve operational enhancements, including the allocation of 24/7 reading rooms, creation of digital discussion cubicles, and installation of automated RFID kiosks.

2. MANDATORY BADGE DISPLAY AND MULTI-FACILITY ACCESS (POL-ID-CARD)

2.1 Mandatory Physical Display: Enrolled students and staff must visibly wear their authenticated Demo University smart RFID identity badge on an official university lanyard at all times while within university boundaries, academic complexes, dining halls, and transport networks.
2.2 Multi-Facility Integration: The embedded MIFARE RFID chip functions as the master access token integrated with CampusPass (SYS-PASS):
- Main gate speed gates and perimeter turnstiles;
- Central Library entrance gates and automated RFID book checkout kiosks (LIB-POL-001);
- Hostel residential perimeter turnstiles and biometric night gates (HSTL-REG-001);
- Commuter transit verification terminals on university buses (TRANS-POL-001).
2.3 Prohibition of Lending and Proxy Access: Lending, borrowing, or fabricating student ID badges to facilitate unauthorized campus entry, proxy classroom attendance on AttendEdge (POL-ATTEND), or fraudulent mess access is a severe breach of student discipline referred to the Proctorial Board (STU-POL-005).

4. DESIGNATED PARKING ZONING AND ACCESS RESTRICTIONS

4.1 West Gate Multi-Level Facility: Primary parking hub for day-scholar student four-wheelers and commuter two-wheelers;
4.2 East Enclave Parking Bays: Designated strictly for hostel residents and residential faculty;
4.3 Chanakya and Ramanujan Administrative Porches: Reserved exclusively for designated executive vehicles, emergency services, and official campus buses;
4.4 Prohibited Zones: Parking in front of building fire exits, emergency hydrants, disability access ramps, delivery bays, or landscaped verges is strictly forbidden.

5. VIOLATIONS, WHEEL CLAMPING, AND PENALTY SCHEDULE (FIN-POL-002)

Security patrols enforce parking regulations using automated mobile license plate recognition and mechanical wheel clamps:
5.1 First Violation (Improper Parking / Failure to Display Tag): Written warning and a monetary fine of Rs. 200 posted to PayVarsity;
5.2 Second Violation (Overspeeding / Reckless Driving / Clamped Vehicle): Mandatory wheel-clamping, fine of Rs. 500, and requirement to execute a written undertaking at the Security Complex;
5.3 Chronic Violations (Three or More Offenses): Total cancellation of campus vehicular access privileges for the remainder of the academic year, with vehicle trespass notices served to external gates.

3. EVACUATION DIRECTIVES AND CODE OF CONDUCT

Upon activation of the evacuation alarm, all building occupants must execute immediate, orderly evacuation:
3.1 Cessation of Activity: Instructors, students, and staff must cease all laboratory apparatus, close chemical fume hoods, shut off primary gas valves, and leave personal baggage behind;
3.2 Prohibition of Lifts/Elevators: Under no circumstances may elevators or freight lifts be utilized during a fire evacuation; all descent must occur via designated external fire escape stairwells;
3.3 Orderly Movement: Move swiftly without running, pushing, or overtaking; assist individuals with physical disabilities or mobility impairments;
3.4 Floor Safety Wardens: Appointed Department Safety Wardens sweep classrooms, restrooms, and research bays to ensure complete clearance before exiting last.

4. DESIGNATED CAMPUS EMERGENCY ASSEMBLY POINTS

Evacuees must proceed directly along marked exit pathways to designated open-air assembly zones:
4.1 Assembly Area Alpha: Central Cricket Ground (For occupants of Ramanujan Block, Chanakya Bhavan, and Admin Wing);
4.2 Assembly Area Beta: East Enclave Central Lawn (For occupants of all student residential hostels and East Dining Hall);
4.3 Assembly Area Gamma: Student Activity Centre Plaza (For occupants of Turing Complex, Central Computing Centre, and Mechanical Workshops).

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (IR)

Within Demo University systems and institutional documentation, the acronym 'IR' represents three entirely separate institutional concepts:
2.1 Incident Report (IR under DEPT-SEC / SEC-SOP-003): The official security documentation logging theft, physical violence, unauthorized trespassing, substance abuse, vandalism, or campus safety breaches;
2.2 Institutional Repository (IR under DEPT-LIB / LIB-POL-004): The digital research library archive maintaining dissertations, doctoral theses, and faculty scholarship;
2.3 Office of International Relations (IR under DEPT-IRO / IRO-POL-001): The administrative division overseeing international student mobility, foreign semester exchanges, and global institutional MOUs.
- Routing Disambiguation Rule: Searches concerning stolen items, property loss, physical harassment, or security guard responses map strictly to the Security Incident Report (SEC-SOP-003).

3. COMMUTER FEE STRUCTURE AND PAYMENT MODES (FIN-POL-001)

3.1 Distance-Tiered Fee Schedule: Annual commuter bus fees are categorized by radial distance zones:
- Zone A (0 to 10 km from campus): Rs. 18,000 per academic year;
- Zone B (11 to 20 km from campus): Rs. 24,000 per academic year;
- Zone C (21 km and above): Rs. 30,000 per academic year.
3.2 Payment Gateway: Bus fees must be settled via the PayVarsity financial portal (SYS-PAY) in full prior to semester commencement or in two equal semester installments.

2. INITIAL BUS PASS ENROLLMENT WORKFLOW (REL-016)

Students seeking commuter bus services must execute the following four-step registration protocol:
2.1 Step 1 (Portal Selection): Log in to the TransPass transit portal at https://transpass.demo.edu.internal using UniCampus ERP credentials. Navigate to 'New Bus Pass Application';
2.2 Step 2 (Route and Stop Selection): Review the interactive route timetable; select the appropriate Route Number (Routes 1–12) and the exact morning/evening pickup stop nearest to the student's verified residence;
2.3 Step 3 (Fee Payment): The system automatically computes the zonal fee. The student completes online payment through PayVarsity (SYS-PAY) via UPI, net banking, or debit card. An authenticated digital receipt is generated immediately;
2.4 Step 4 (RFID Sync and SLA): Upon payment confirmation, TransPass transmits authorized transit credentials to CampusPass within twenty-four (24) working hours. The student's physical Smart RFID ID Card is activated automatically for handheld scanner tap-in on the selected route.

3. ROUTE AND PICKUP STOP MODIFICATION WORKFLOW

Students relocating their residence mid-semester may request a route or stop adjustment:
3.1 Eligibility: Route change requests are accepted only during the first ten (10) working days of each semester, or upon submission of an authenticated proof of residential address change (e.g., parental rental agreement);
3.2 Application on TransPass: The student submits a 'Route Change Request' on TransPass specifying the current route, proposed new route, and requested pickup node;
3.3 Differential Fee Adjustment: If transitioning to a higher fee zone, the difference must be cleared on PayVarsity within 48 hours; if moving to a lower fee zone, no mid-term differential refund is issued;
3.4 Processing SLA: The updated route privilege is reprogrammed onto the RFID card within two (2) working days of supervisor sign-off.

4. PASS CANCELLATION AND FEE REFUND NORMS (FIN-POL-004)

If a student relocates into campus residential hostels or withdraws from the university, bus pass refunds are processed under the following statutory refund tiers:
4.1 Cancellation Prior to Class Commencement: Ninety percent (90%) refund of the assessed annual transport fee, with 10% retained as an administrative processing surcharge;
4.2 Cancellation Within First 15 Calendar Days of Semester: Fifty percent (50%) refund of the assessed semester fee;
4.3 Cancellation After 15 Calendar Days: Zero refund, except in cases of certified medical leaves of absence sanctioned by the Dean of Academic Affairs or formal institutional withdrawal (POL-TRANSFER-CERT);
4.4 Refund Credit: Approved refunds are credited directly to the student's verified bank account registered in PayVarsity within fourteen (14) working days.

3. SERVICE REQUEST LODGING VIA RESOLVEDESK

All facility defects, breakdowns, and repair requisitions must be formally registered on ResolveDesk (SYS-RESOLVE) at https://resolvedesk.demo.edu.internal under Category: Campus Operations:
3.1 Civil Maintenance: Broken floor tiles, masonry cracks, window pane replacements, door hardware, carpentry, and classroom desks;
3.2 Plumbing and Sanitation: Restroom leaks, clogged drainage, overhead tank overflows, and drinking water dispenser filter replacements;
3.3 Electrical Infrastructure: Power point failures, classroom lighting, ceiling fans, circuit breaker trips, and localized UPS backup faults;
3.4 Housekeeping and Grounds: Waste collection, common area sanitization, tree pruning, and estate pest control.

4. PRIORITY CLASSIFICATIONS AND SERVICE-LEVEL AGREEMENTS (SLAS)

Maintenance requisitions are triaged into three response tiers:
4.1 Tier 1 (Emergency Priority - 4-Hour Response SLA): Major electrical short circuits, burst primary water mains, sewer blockages, or hazardous structural ceiling collapses. Immediate dispatch of on-duty emergency technicians;
4.2 Tier 2 (Urgent Priority - 24 to 48-Hour Response SLA): Classroom projection power faults, air-conditioning failure in active lecture halls, broken entrance door locks, or restroom fixture leaks;
4.3 Tier 3 (Routine Priority - 5 Working Days SLA): Aesthetic touch-up painting, general carpentry adjustments, planned furniture reallocations, and routine landscaping.

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (CR)

Within university administrative taxonomy, the acronym 'CR' possesses three distinct, non-overlapping meanings:
2.1 Conference Room (CR under DEPT-OPS / OPS-FAC-003): The bookable institutional venue designed for committee meetings, guest symposiums, and administrative deliberations (e.g., CR-1, CR-2 in Turing Complex, Ramanujan Executive Hall);
2.2 Class Representative (CR under DEPT-DSA / STU-LDR-001): The elected student representative serving as cohort liaison between students, course instructors, and academic departments;
2.3 Course Registration (CR under DEPT-ACAD / ACAD-POL-001): The statutory academic process of enrolling in semester courses and electives on UniCampus ERP.
- Cross-Domain Disambiguation Note: General institutional Conference Rooms (OPS-FAC-003) must not be confused with Library Discussion Cubicles (LIB-POL-003), which are bookable strictly for student study groups through LibNet.

3. CAMPUS VENUE INVENTORY AND AMENITIES

3.1 Conference Rooms (CR-1 to CR-6): Seating capacities of 20 to 50 persons, equipped with motorized projection screens, executive teleconference cameras, localized air conditioning (OPS-FAC-005), and interactive smartboards;
3.2 Departmental Seminar Halls: Capacities of 100 to 150 tiered seats located in Ramanujan and Turing complexes, equipped with dual projection systems and wireless lapel microphones;
3.3 Newton Central Auditorium: Premier 800-seat performance venue equipped with line-array acoustics, stage theatrical lighting, backstage green rooms, and dedicated 3-phase generator power.

4. RESERVATION PROCEDURES AND TIMELINES (REL-008)

4.1 Faculty and Academic Requisitions: Department Heads and faculty organizers must submit venue requisitions on ResolveDesk at least three (3) working days in advance;
4.2 Student Club and Society Requisitions: Recognized student clubs must submit venue requests accompanied by the formal SAC Event Sanction letter (SAC-POL-001) at least ten (10) working days prior to the event;
4.3 Priority Allocation Hierarchy: If scheduling conflicts emerge, space is allocated strictly under the following statutory hierarchy:
- Statutory Apex Meetings (Executive Council, Academic Council, Board of Governors);
- Centralized Examinations and University Placement Drives (DEPT-CDC / DEPT-EXAM);
- Official University Conferences, Symposia, and Accreditation Audits;
- Recognized Student Club Co-Curricular Competitions and Fests.

5. USAGE NORMS, CLEANLINESS, AND DAMAGE RESTITUTION

5.1 Consumables Restriction: Consuming meals, open beverages, or snacks inside auditoriums and conference rooms is strictly prohibited; refreshments must be served in designated outdoor foyers or dining courtyards;
5.2 Audiovisual Handover: Authorized AV technicians conduct pre-event and post-event equipment diagnostics. Organizers are held financially responsible under POL-DISCIPLINE for any physical damage to projection optics, acoustic microphones, or stage equipment caused by negligence.

3. MANDATORY STATUTORY TEMPERATURE SETPOINTS

In compliance with national energy efficiency directives and the university's environmental sustainability charter:
3.1 Standard Temperature Band: Thermostats across all administrative offices, staff rooms, conference halls, and teaching lecture theaters must be maintained strictly between twenty-four degrees Celsius (24°C) and twenty-six degrees Celsius (26°C);
3.2 Prohibition of Overcooling: Tampering with thermostat lockboxes or lowering room temperatures below 22°C is strictly forbidden;
3.3 Doors and Windows Mandate: Exterior doors, corridor windows, and ventilation louvers must remain fully closed while air-conditioning systems are engaged to prevent thermal loss and compressor overload.

4. OPERATING SCHEDULES ACROSS CAMPUS ZONES

4.1 Academic and Administrative Blocks: Central HVAC chillers operate on scheduled lecture days from 08:30 AM to 05:30 PM. Units are shut down automatically overnight and on weekends, except for authorized special events (OPS-FAC-003);
4.2 Continuous Mission-Critical Cooling: Data center server vaults (Turing Complex Server Room IT-SYS-001), high-performance computing clusters, and Central Library 24/7 digital hub maintain 24/7 dedicated precision air-conditioning with redundant backup chillers;
4.3 Residential Hostels (Nilgiri AC Wing): Air-conditioning operates from 08:00 PM to 07:00 AM on weekdays and 01:00 PM to 05:00 PM on weekends. Enrolled residents pay authorized supplemental utility electricity fees via PayVarsity under HSTL-REG-001.

5. PROHIBITED APPLIANCES AND BREAKDOWN REPORTING

5.1 Prohibited Private Appliances: In accordance with HSTL-POL-003, private high-power electrical appliances—including portable space heaters, immersion water rods, and personal air coolers—are strictly banned in student dormitories due to electrical fire hazard;
5.2 Maintenance Reporting: Compressor failures, unusual vibrating noises, water drippage, or refrigerant leaks must be logged immediately on ResolveDesk under Category: Campus Operations > HVAC for resolution within 24 hours.

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (SE)

Within university administrative nomenclature, the abbreviation 'SE' denotes three distinct entities across engineering, examinations, and facilities:
2.1 Site Engineer (SE under DEPT-OPS / OPS-EST-004): The full-time civil engineering professional responsible for structural safety, estate maintenance, contractor supervision, and physical building inspections;
2.2 Software Engineering (SE under DEPT-CSE / ACAD-CRS-005): The undergraduate computer science course (CSE305) covering agile development methodologies and software lifecycle design;
2.3 Semester Exam (SE under DEPT-EXAM / EXAM-GUI-002): The informal colloquial term used by students when querying Semester End Examinations (SEE).
- Disambiguation Instruction: Searches mentioning structural repairs, ceiling cracks, roofing leaks, carpentry, concrete testing, or contractor oversight point exclusively to the Site Engineer (OPS-EST-004).

3. ROUTINE CIVIL INFRASTRUCTURE INSPECTION DUTIES

Estate Site Engineers execute structured daily, weekly, and monthly surveillance routines across the physical campus estate:
3.1 Structural Integrity Monitoring: Inspecting foundations, load-bearing pillars, beams, expansion joints, and external brick masonry across Ramanujan Block, Chanakya Bhavan, Turing Complex, and Nilgiri Hostels for hairline cracks, water seepage, or structural fatigue;
3.2 Roofing, Parapets, and Drainage: Conducting monthly pre-monsoon and post-monsoon audits of rooftop waterproofing membranes, rainwater drainage spouts, overhead water reservoir supports, and parapet railings;
3.3 Fire Stairwells and Emergency Egress: Inspecting all external fire escape stairways, safety handrails, illuminated exit signage, and fire door hardware weekly to guarantee unhindered egress under SEC-SOP-001;
3.4 Perimeter Security Enclosures: Auditing perimeter boundary walls, razor-wire security toppings, vehicle crash barriers, and stormwater culverts in coordination with Campus Security (DEPT-SEC).

4. CONTRACTOR SUPERVISION AND QUALITY AUDITING

4.1 Quality Compliance: Supervising civil, plumbing, painting, and carpentry contractors engaged in university repair or capital expansion projects; ensuring raw materials conform to national civil engineering codes;
4.2 Measurement and Billing Verification: Reviewing contractor measurement sheets, conducting destructive/non-destructive material tests, and validating completion milestones before recommending fiscal sign-off to the Director of Campus Operations.

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY AND ENTITY DISAMBIGUATION (TA)

Within the university nomenclature system, the abbreviation 'TA' corresponds to three distinct institutional concepts:
2.1 Technical Assistant (TA under DEPT-OPS / OPS-LAB-003): The full-time permanent technical staff member responsible for maintaining hardware laboratories, machine tooling, instrument calibrations, and student workshop safety;
2.2 Teaching Assistant (TA under DEPT-ACAD / ACAD-POL-007): The graduate M.Tech or Ph.D. scholar appointed to assist course professors with student tutorials, lab code grading, and remedial mentoring;
2.3 Travel Allowance (TA under DEPT-FIN / FIN-POL-006): The financial reimbursement per diem granted to faculty and students for authorized official travel and academic conference deputation.
- Disambiguation Note: Requests regarding machine repairs, workshop tools, component issuance, or lab technician signatures refer to the Technical Assistant (OPS-LAB-003).

3. CORE OPERATIONAL DUTIES OF TECHNICAL ASSISTANTS

3.1 Laboratory Readiness and Calibrations: Conducting pre-semester inspections, electrical machine testing, fluid mechanics sensor calibrations, surveying equipment servicing, and chemical reagent stock auditing before instructional classes begin;
3.2 Student Supervision and PPE Enforcement: Enforcing mandatory laboratory safety protocols (ACAD-LAB-001)—including the mandatory wearing of protective safety goggles, lab coats, and closed industrial footwear; operating primary emergency stop switches if hazards arise;
3.3 Component Issuance and Inventory Control: Managing daily stock registers for hardware components, microcontrollers, measurement probes, and mechanical tooling; collecting component return sign-offs before the conclusion of each lab period;
3.4 Equipment Damage and Breakage Logging (REL-018): If student equipment is accidentally shorted, dropped, or damaged, the Technical Assistant drafts a preliminary Equipment Incident Slip logging machine serial number, operating conditions, and student details for review by the Laboratory Faculty In-Charge.
